{
  "note": "Neurotransmitter Receptors",
  "source": "30c39efcb6a2",
  "script": "0a35bba0591d",
  "voice": "Jessica Gallagher - Clear and Neutral",
  "model": "eleven_v4",
  "fingerprint": "9a6263d4c54c",
  "duration": 221.257,
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Neurotransmitter Receptors",
      "start": 0,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Once a neurotransmitter has crossed the synaptic cleft, it binds to receptors on the postsynaptic membrane.",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 6.6
        },
        {
          "text": "Each of these receptors falls into one of two types, ionotropic or metabotropic.",
          "start": 6.88,
          "end": 12.96
        },
        {
          "text": "In what follows, we'll look at how each type works, and after that at why both of them exist.",
          "start": 13.04,
          "end": 19.12
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Ionotropic Receptors",
      "start": 19.958,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Ionotropic receptors come first.",
          "start": 19.958,
          "end": 22.718
        },
        {
          "text": "Each one is a single membrane protein, made of several protein subunits arranged around a central pore.",
          "start": 22.758,
          "end": 29.918
        },
        {
          "text": "The transmitter binding site sits on the outer face of the same protein that forms the channel.",
          "start": 29.958,
          "end": 35.678
        },
        {
          "text": "In other words, the receptor and the channel are one molecule.",
          "start": 35.718,
          "end": 39.851
        },
        {
          "text": "Here's how it works.",
          "start": 39.905,
          "end": 41.371
        },
        {
          "text": "When the transmitter binds, the subunits change shape and the pore opens.",
          "start": 41.425,
          "end": 46.518
        },
        {
          "text": "Ions then flow straight through, down their electrochemical gradients, with no intermediate steps.",
          "start": 46.598,
          "end": 52.878
        },
        {
          "text": "The channel stays open only while the transmitter is bound, and transmitter is cleared from the cleft quickly.",
          "start": 52.918,
          "end": 59.371
        },
        {
          "text": "The current therefore lasts milliseconds.",
          "start": 59.425,
          "end": 62.798
        },
        {
          "text": "Now for the standard examples.",
          "start": 62.838,
          "end": 64.958
        },
        {
          "text": "One is the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor.",
          "start": 64.998,
          "end": 68.318
        },
        {
          "text": "Two more are glutamate receptors, namely AMPA and NMDA.",
          "start": 68.358,
          "end": 73.358
        },
        {
          "text": "Their names stand for alpha-amino-three-hydroxy-five-methyl-four-isoxazolepropionic acid and N-methyl-D-aspartate.",
          "start": 73.398,
          "end": 83.518
        },
        {
          "text": "Last of all come the GABA type A and glycine receptors, GABA being short for gamma-aminobutyric acid.",
          "start": 83.558,
          "end": 91.158
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Metabotropic Receptors",
      "start": 92.003,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Metabotropic receptors work differently, since here the receptor and the ion channel are separate proteins.",
          "start": 92.003,
          "end": 99.163
        },
        {
          "text": "The receptor acts through a G protein, a three part switch with alpha, beta and gamma subunits, sitting on the inner face of the membrane.",
          "start": 99.203,
          "end": 108.603
        },
        {
          "text": "Let's follow the mechanism step by step.",
          "start": 108.643,
          "end": 111.403
        },
        {
          "text": "It begins when the transmitter binds the receptor on the outside.",
          "start": 111.443,
          "end": 115.656
        },
        {
          "text": "The activated receptor then switches on the G protein, and the alpha subunit separates from beta and gamma and moves along the membrane.",
          "start": 115.71,
          "end": 124.523
        },
        {
          "text": "Next, the alpha subunit activates an effector protein, which is an enzyme.",
          "start": 124.563,
          "end": 129.816
        },
        {
          "text": "This effector generates intracellular messengers, also called second messengers.",
          "start": 129.87,
          "end": 135.243
        },
        {
          "text": "Finally, the messengers act on the ion channel from the inside, opening or closing it, and ions flow.",
          "start": 135.283,
          "end": 142.43
        },
        {
          "text": "This indirect design has three consequences.",
          "start": 142.536,
          "end": 145.816
        },
        {
          "text": "The first is slow activation.",
          "start": 145.87,
          "end": 148.403
        },
        {
          "text": "Several protein to protein steps take place before any ion moves, which is why the response takes from tens of milliseconds to seconds to begin.",
          "start": 148.483,
          "end": 157.816
        },
        {
          "text": "The second is a long duration of action.",
          "start": 157.87,
          "end": 160.763
        },
        {
          "text": "The messengers persist inside the cell after the transmitter has left the receptor, and as a result the effect lasts seconds to minutes.",
          "start": 160.803,
          "end": 169.243
        },
        {
          "text": "The third is amplification.",
          "start": 169.283,
          "end": 171.683
        },
        {
          "text": "One receptor can activate many G proteins, and each effector makes many messenger molecules.",
          "start": 171.763,
          "end": 178.043
        },
        {
          "text": "That way, the signal is magnified and can spread through the whole cell.",
          "start": 178.083,
          "end": 182.563
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Why both exist?",
      "start": 182.988,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "So why do both types exist?",
          "start": 182.988,
          "end": 185.468
        },
        {
          "text": "Ionotropic receptors give speed and precision.",
          "start": 185.628,
          "end": 189.108
        },
        {
          "text": "They convert the presynaptic signal into a postsynaptic potential almost instantly, and they are ideal for rapid, point to point transmission.",
          "start": 189.148,
          "end": 198.388
        },
        {
          "text": "Metabotropic receptors, on the other hand, trade speed for reach.",
          "start": 198.428,
          "end": 203.095
        },
        {
          "text": "They are slow and long lasting.",
          "start": 203.121,
          "end": 205.735
        },
        {
          "text": "Because they work through diffusible messengers, they tune the overall excitability and chemistry of the cell, rather than simply firing it.",
          "start": 205.841,
          "end": 214.241
        },
        {
          "text": "To sum up, there are two kinds of neurotransmitter receptor, one built for speed and the other for reach.",
          "start": 214.295,
          "end": 221.308
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "segments": [
