    {"duration":90.98449,"alignment":{"characters":"Metabotropic receptors work differently, since here the receptor and the ion channel are separate proteins. The receptor acts through a G protein, a three part switch with alpha, beta and gamma subunits, sitting on the inner face of the membrane. Let's follow the mechanism step by step. It begins when the transmitter binds the receptor on the outside. The activated receptor then switches on the G protein, and the alpha subunit separates from beta and gamma and moves along the membrane. Next, the alpha subunit activates an effector protein, which is an enzyme. This effector generates intracellular messengers, also called second messengers. Finally, the messengers act on the ion channel from the inside, opening or closing it, and ions flow. This indirect design has three consequences. The first is slow activation. Several protein to protein steps take place before any ion moves, which is why the response takes from tens of milliseconds to seconds to begin. The second is a long duration of action. The messengers persist inside the cell after the transmitter has left the receptor, and as a result the effect lasts seconds to minutes. The third is amplification. One receptor can activate many G proteins, and each effector makes many messenger molecules. That way, the signal is magnified and can spread through the whole cell.","starts":[0,53,107,160,280,400,469,537,606,674,743,811,880,960,1000,1040,1097,1154,1211,1269,1326,1383,1440,1520,1580,1640,1700,1760,1840,1920,1936,1952,1968,1984,2000,2128,2256,2384,2512,2640,2680,2720,2768,2816,2864,2912,2960,3040,3140,3240,3340,3440,3520,3533,3547,3560,3600,3680,3760,3827,3893,3960,4027,4093,4160,4240,4267,4293,4320,4400,4427,4453,4480,4560,4640,4720,4800,4880,4926,4971,5017,5063,5109,5154,5200,5280,5333,5387,5440,5520,5600,5680,5720,5760,5800,5840,5880,5920,6040,6080,6120,6160,6200,6240,6533,6827,7120,7160,7200,7213,7227,7240,7280,7360,7440,7507,7573,7640,7707,7773,7840,7920,798