    {"duration":38.269388,"alignment":{"characters":"So why do both types exist? Ionotropic receptors give speed and precision. They convert the presynaptic signal into a postsynaptic potential almost instantly, and they are ideal for rapid, point to point transmission. Metabotropic receptors, on the other hand, trade speed for reach. They are slow and long lasting. Because they work through diffusible messengers, they tune the overall excitability and chemistry of the cell, rather than simply firing it. To sum up, there are two kinds of neurotransmitter receptor, one built for speed and the other for reach.","starts":[0,40,80,240,320,400,480,600,660,720,840,870,900,930,960,1040,1104,1168,1232,1296,1360,1640,1696,1752,1808,1864,1920,2480,2640,2693,2747,2800,2869,2937,3006,3074,3143,3211,3280,3360,3400,3440,3509,3577,3646,3714,3783,3851,3920,4000,4060,4120,4180,4240,4480,4528,4576,4624,4672,4720,4800,4853,4907,4960,5520,5582,5644,5707,5769,5831,5893,5956,6018,6080,6120,6160,6180,6200,6220,6240,6320,6377,6434,6491,6549,6606,6663,6720,6800,6827,6853,6880,7040,7080,7120,7160,7200,7280,7360,7408,7456,7504,7552,7600,7680,7760,7840,7920,8000,8080,8160,8280,8310,8340,8370,8400,8560,8667,8773,8795,8816,8837,8859,8880,8920,8960,9024,9088,9152,9216,9280,9640,9680,9720,9760,9800,9840,9880,9920,9960,10000,10240,10280,10320,10360,10400,10440,10480,10560,10617,10674,10731,10789,10846,10903,10960,11240,11520,11547,11573,11582,11591,11600,11680,11700,11720,11740,11760,11840,11867,11893,11920,12000,12096,12192,12288,12384,12480,12560,12613,12667,12720,12800,12880,12960,13040,13120,13200,13360,13440,13472,13504,13536,13568,13600,13680,13720,13760,13840,13888,13936,13984,14032,14080,14240,14272,14304,14336,14368,14400,14537,14674,14811,14949,15086,15223,15360,15400,15440,15520,15600,15680,15760,15840,15897,15954,16011,16069,16126,16183,16240,16320,16360,16400,16469,16537,16606,16674,16743,16811,16880,16920,16960,17000,17040,17080,17093,17107,17120,17240,17264,17288,17312,17336,17360,17